<urn:atm:triage:finding/AI14-QA1-RBQA-F001>
  triage:status "scope-mismatch" ;
  triage:dispatchReady false ;
  triage:resolutionGuidance "HOLD -- do not implement either 'remove UDS' or 'remove TCP' as previously guided (both directions were dispatched and retracted). docs/requirements.md REQ-CORE-TRANSPORT-001 states: 'Unix same-host clients use HTTP over UDS and may use HTTP over loopback TCP; Windows same-host clients use HTTP over loopback TCP only' and explicitly requires 'Unix/Windows parity requires equivalent local HTTP request/response tests: UDS plus loopback TCP on Unix and loopback TCP on Windows.' This means concurrent UDS+TCP-loopback binding on Unix is the DOCUMENTED, REQUIRED contract, not dead/violating infrastructure. The finding's premise ('dead/unreferenced-by-any-client production infrastructure kept alive, not removed', 'Single-receive-path boundary violation') conflicts with this requirement and needs re-triage against REQ-CORE-TRANSPORT-001 before any dispatch. Client-side (atm-daemon-client) currently resolving only via local_http_record_path may itself be the real gap (missing UDS-path client support), not the server binding both listeners. Needs fresh triage, not implementation." ;
  triage:correctionNote "2026-07-26 (quality-mgr): QA-1 dispatched against PR #665 (commit 780a8870, 'fix/legacy-uds-tcp-shared-client'), which attempts exactly the gap-closure this record's resolutionGuidance called for (client-side UDS support via a shared atm-daemon-client transport). Full 6-reviewer QA-1 set run independently, not by self-report. VERDICT: FAIL. Architecture/boundary-discipline of the shared-client approach is sound (ruthless-boundary-qa, req-qa both PASS on the core design), but the commit as pushed is not mergeable: (1) arch-qa BLOCKING/RULE-012 -- adding `interprocess` as a mandatory dependency of atm-daemon-client transitively reintroduces the forbidden `atm-graft -> interprocess` edge into atm-graft's default production transport (boundaries/atm-graft/shared-client-consumer.toml forbidden_edges, previously fixed under AI15-QA1-BLOCKING-01), and the boundary lint doesn't catch it because it only scans direct Cargo.toml edges, not transitive ones -- a real enforcement bypass. (2) rust-service-hardening-agent CRITICAL/RSH-001 -- UDS connect failures (NotFound/PermissionDenied/ConnectionRefused) all collapse into one identical operator-facing message because AtmError::Display drops .cause, defeating this commit's own stated diagnostic goal. (3) rust-qa-agent CRITICAL x2 -- QA-001: try_connect's return-type change (TcpStream -> LocalDaemonConnection) breaks the Windows compile of atm-daemon's test target (type mismatch in test_support.rs); QA-002: bisection-confirmed regression, test hardcodes UDS filename \"daemon.sock\" while the real client now resolves \"atm-daemon.sock\" via HOST_RUNTIME_SOCKET_FILE. (4) Independently reproduced in CI: Windows lint leg fails to compile (unconditional `use std::io::Write as _;` unused on non-unix, -D warnings), Ubuntu Test leg fails on the same test QA-002 identified. Do NOT close this finding or AI11-QA5-RBQA-F001 -- the real fix direction identified in this record's resolutionGuidance is confirmed correct, but this specific commit is not the resolution yet. FAIL verdict sent to team-lead; QA report posted to PR #665." .

<urn:atm:triage:finding/AI14-QA1-RBQA-F001>
  triage:correctionNote "2026-07-27 (quality-mgr): QA-2 dispatched against PR #665 fix-round-2 (commit 7a49a7f4). Independently re-verified all 7 review targets, not by self-report. VERDICT: FAIL. Six of seven targets are genuinely SATISFIED this round: (a) RULE-012 -- boundaries/atm-graft/shared-client-consumer.toml gained an explicit no-direct-interprocess note, backed by a real enforced test (test_graft_shared_client_has_no_direct_interprocess_dependency in .just/tests/test_lint_boundaries.py) asserting both no direct Cargo.toml edge and no collect_boundary_violations() hit; this is a deliberate narrowing to direct-edge-only enforcement (transitive reintroduction via atm-daemon-client is still technically present in the real dependency graph but is now explicitly the accepted architecture per the review target's own framing of a team-lead ruling), not an unauthorized bypass. (b) RSH-001 -- AtmError::daemon_unavailable_with_cause used at every UDS/TCP failure site, verified via a real test asserting error.cause() is non-empty. (c) UDS/TCP timeout-unsupported degrade parity -- confirmed via the single shared apply_local_ipc_deadline helper used by both transports. (d) QA-002 hardcoded socket filename -- fixed, HOST_RUNTIME_SOCKET_FILE used throughout. (e) ubuntu/macos CI legs and local lint+test all green. BUT (f) QA-001 Windows compile break is CONFIRMED STILL BROKEN, verified via live Windows CI (not code inspection): Just lint (windows-latest) fails with 'error: unused import: try_connect_with_transport -- could not compile atm-daemon-client (lib test)' (https://github.com/randlee/atm-core/actions/runs/30234256319/job/89878850611). Root cause: the mod tests use-list in crates/atm-daemon-client/src/lib.rs imports try_connect_with_transport unconditionally, but it is only referenced by the new #[cfg(unix)]-gated test uds_connect_failure_keeps_the_socket_cause_in_the_displayed_error -- same defect class as the original QA-001 finding, freshly reintroduced by this round's own new test. Do NOT close this finding or AI11-QA5-RBQA-F001 -- architecture now looks resolved (client is UDS-preferred by default on Unix, verified with a real bound-listener integration test) but the commit does not compile on Windows. FAIL verdict sent to team-lead and directly to arch-ctm with the exact fix (gate the import under #[cfg(unix)]); QA report posted to PR #665. arch-ctm acknowledged (msg 01KYGSPDJ3SDMQ7170QJYV1J2P) and pushed a follow-up commit 5ef43130 gating the import -- QA-3 pending against that commit." .
